# Korvane Cold Chain Sp. z o.o.
## Annual Report 2023 — extract: Management Discussion and Analysis and Financial Review

Registered office: ul. Łużycka 14, 81-537 Gdynia · KRS 0000488213 · NIP 586-217-40-93

This extract reproduces the narrative sections of the Annual Report for the financial year ended 31 December 2023 that the Board resolved to publish on 12 April 2024. It is not a substitute for the full statutory financial statements, which together with the auditor's report were filed with the National Court Register in accordance with the Accounting Act. Where this extract and the full financial statements differ, the financial statements prevail. Figures are presented in EUR million (EUR m) unless stated otherwise; the Group's presentation currency has been the euro since 1 January 2020, even though the parent company keeps its statutory books in PLN. Totals may not add up exactly because of rounding. Forward-looking statements reflect management's expectations at the date of approval and are not guarantees.

## 1. Letter from the Chief Executive Officer

Dear shareholders, customers, colleagues and — yes — the people at the quay who read these things for fun,

When Dieter and I started this company in a rented office above a tyre shop in Gdynia in 2011, the business plan fitted on four pages and the word "ColdLedger" had not yet been invented, which in hindsight was a happier time. Twelve years later the plan is considerably longer and the billing system is considerably more complicated, but the idea has not changed: put a refrigerated box on a ship, and make sure that what comes out at the other end is as cold as what went in.

2023 was the year in which that idea became a four-country operation that happens to have a fifth, tiny, extremely windy yard at Kłaipėda. Group revenue rose to EUR 97.6m from EUR 84.3m, an increase of 15.8%, and EBITDA reached EUR 17.9m, a rise of 20.9% on 2022. Net profit was EUR 4.7m. I am proud of those numbers, but I am prouder of three others that rarely make it into a letter like this one: we handled 612,400 lift moves in the year without a single reportable injury at the Mothership, we closed the KC-Tag 3 handover window on schedule, and our pharma customers recorded a 99.64% excursion-free delivery rate across the 14 GDP contracts we now hold.

It was not an easy year. Electricity was still expensive in the first half, the Kłaipėda yard cost us more than we told the Board it would, and the KC-Tag 3 end-of-life programme turned out to involve rather more batteries than anyone had counted. Kamil's team spent most of November explaining to us what a "legacy tag handover window" is. I will spare you the details but I would like to record my gratitude.

For 2024 we expect revenue between EUR 104m and EUR 108m and an EBITDA of EUR 19.0m to EUR 20.0m; the Board has approved a capital expenditure envelope of up to EUR 19.5m, a large part of which goes to the refresh of our refrigeration fleet and to plug capacity at Rotterdam-Maasvlakte. We intend to continue to pay out between a quarter and a third of net profit as dividend, and the Board will recommend a distribution of EUR 1.5m for 2023 to the Ordinary Shareholders' Meeting on 27 May 2024.

Thank you.

Marta Kowarzik
Chief Executive Officer, Gdynia, 12 April 2024

## 2. Financial review (Chief Financial Officer)

### 2.1 Headline results

I will try to write this the way I would explain it to Marta on the way to the airport, which is to say with the tables at the back and the story at the front. The Group's results for 2023 compared with 2022 are summarised below.

| EUR m | 2023 | 2022 | Change |
| Revenue | 97.6 | 84.3 | +15.8% |
| EBITDA | 17.9 | 14.8 | +20.9% |
| EBITDA margin | 18.3% | 17.6% | +0.7 pp |
| Depreciation and amortisation | 8.5 | 7.7 | +10.4% |
| Operating profit (EBIT) | 9.4 | 7.1 | +32.4% |
| Net finance costs | 3.1 | 2.1 | +47.6% |
| Profit before tax | 6.3 | 5.0 | +26.0% |
| Income tax | 1.6 | 1.2 | +33.3% |
| Net profit | 4.7 | 3.8 | +23.7% |

Revenue growth came from all four segments, though in uneven proportions, as the segment review in section 3 explains. Reefer Services remains more than half of the Group at 53.6% of revenue (2022: 56.8%), and its share fell not because it shrank but because Pharma Lanes and Monitoring & Software grew faster. The EBITDA margin improved by 0.7 percentage points to 18.3%. Two items deserve a mention because they flatter or burden the comparison: first, a one-off write-down of KC-Tag 3 battery inventory of EUR 214,000 was booked in December after discussion with the auditors, and second, the 2022 comparative contains EUR 0.35m of start-up costs for the Rotterdam depot that did not repeat.

Depreciation and amortisation rose to EUR 8.5m, made up of EUR 6.1m depreciation of property, plant and equipment, EUR 2.0m depreciation of right-of-use assets and EUR 0.4m amortisation of intangible assets. Net finance costs grew by almost half, which is entirely a function of interest rates; the breakdown is EUR 2.5m interest on borrowings, EUR 0.8m interest on lease liabilities, EUR 0.2m net foreign exchange loss, less EUR 0.4m of interest income on cash balances. The effective tax rate was 25.4%, higher than the blended statutory rate because a portion of the Swedish losses carried no deferred tax asset.

Quarterly phasing is shown below since several readers asked for it after the half-year. The second half carries the produce season, and for 2023 the fourth quarter was both the biggest quarter by revenue and by EBITDA.

| EUR m | Q1 2023 | Q2 2023 | Q3 2023 | Q4 2023 |
| Revenue | 22.9 | 23.3 | 25.5 | 25.9 |
| EBITDA | 3.9 | 4.2 | 4.5 | 5.3 |

[[chart bar "Group revenue 2019-2023" | 2019=52.4, 2020=56.9, 2021=68.8, 2022=84.3, 2023=97.6 | unit=EUR m]]

*Figure 1 — Group revenue by financial year, EUR m. The compound annual growth rate over 2019–2023 is 16.8%.*

### 2.2 Cost structure

Operating costs before depreciation were EUR 79.7m. Direct operating costs, which are everything that moves with the number of boxes and days, were EUR 52.4m; staff costs EUR 22.6m; and other operating expenses EUR 4.7m. Within direct costs the biggest line is subcontracted haulage at EUR 14.9m, followed by electricity at EUR 9.8m, port and terminal fees at EUR 8.4m, repairs and maintenance at EUR 5.2m and fuel at EUR 3.1m; the remaining EUR 11.0m is a mixed bag of container leases, insurance premiums, cloud hosting and consumables that we have, for the time being, not split further.

Electricity deserves a paragraph of its own. We consumed 58,300 MWh in 2023 at an average all-in price of EUR 168.1 per MWh, which meant a total spend 7.5% lower than the year before (EUR 10.6m was spent in 2022) but still roughly double what the same energy cost in 2019. The fixed-price contract we signed for Gdynia expired at the end of June, and the Rotterdam depot buys on the day-ahead market with a small forward collar. As a rule of thumb each EUR 10 per MWh change in the average price moves EBITDA by EUR 0.58m.

### 2.3 Cash flow and balance sheet

Cash flow from operating activities, after interest and tax, was EUR 14.2m. We invested EUR 16.4m in capital expenditure, paid EUR 1.0m of dividend relating to 2022 and signed new leases worth EUR 2.7m, and as a consequence net debt rose by EUR 5.9m to EUR 54.9m. Net debt to EBITDA improved nevertheless from 3.31x to 3.07x because EBITDA grew faster than debt. Interest cover, which is EBITDA divided by net finance costs, was 5.8x.

[[chart line "Net debt / EBITDA by quarter-end" | Q1-22=3.62, Q2-22=3.58, Q3-22=3.44, Q4-22=3.31, Q1-23=3.26, Q2-23=3.19, Q3-23=3.12, Q4-23=3.07 | unit=x]]

*Figure 2 — Net debt to LTM EBITDA at each quarter-end, 2022–2023. Covenant ceiling 3.75x until 31 March 2024, 3.50x thereafter.*

Trade receivables stood at EUR 13.4m, corresponding to 50.1 days of sales outstanding; the expected credit loss allowance is EUR 0.52m and write-offs in the year were EUR 0.09m. Total assets were EUR 131.7m and equity EUR 31.6m, an equity ratio of 24.0%. Property, plant and equipment (EUR 88.4m), right-of-use assets (EUR 21.9m) and intangible assets (EUR 7.6m, of which EUR 3.9m capitalised development) are the bulk of the asset side.

### 2.4 Five-year summary

Some readers prefer the long view, so here it is. Headcount has grown by a factor of 1.6 since 2019, whereas revenue has grown by a factor of 1.86 over the same period, the year in which ColdLedger went live and I joined, and both events were, depending on whom you ask, either coincidence or cause.

| EUR m unless stated | 2019 | 2020 | 2021 | 2022 | 2023 |
| Revenue | 52.4 | 56.9 | 68.8 | 84.3 | 97.6 |
| EBITDA | 9.1 | 10.2 | 12.0 | 14.8 | 17.9 |
| EBITDA margin | 17.4% | 17.9% | 17.4% | 17.6% | 18.3% |
| Net profit | 2.3 | 2.9 | 3.0 | 3.8 | 4.7 |
| FTE at year-end | 188 | 201 | 229 | 273 | 301 |

The 2020 uptick in margin was a pandemic effect: pharma volumes rose sharply while produce was disrupted for about two quarters. The 2021 dip came from the cost of building the Tidewalk v5 platform, most of which was expensed rather than capitalised under the policy then in force.

### 2.5 Alternative performance measures

For the avoidance of doubt, the following definitions are used in this extract and in lender reporting. EBITDA is operating profit before depreciation, amortisation and impairment, and is shown before any item that management classifies as exceptional only if that item exceeds EUR 0.5m; no item exceeded that threshold in 2023, so the EBITDA reported here is also the EBITDA after exceptional items. Net debt means interest-bearing borrowings plus lease liabilities less cash and cash equivalents. LTM means last twelve months. Net debt to EBITDA uses period-end net debt and LTM EBITDA, and the covenant calculation in the facilities agreement differs from the figure in this report only in that it excludes capitalised arrangement fees of EUR 0.3m. Free cash flow, a measure that the Board does not currently use for targets, is not reported.

### 2.6 Result against budget and previous guidance

In December 2022 the Board approved a 2023 budget with revenue of EUR 92.4m and EBITDA of EUR 16.1m. Actual revenue was EUR 5.2m ahead and EBITDA EUR 1.8m ahead, which flatters the budget process more than it should; roughly half of the revenue outperformance came from two things that were not in the plan at all — the early Kłaipėda start in March, and a surge of seafood shipments in the fourth quarter after a hold-up at another Baltic port. The capex budget of EUR 14.8m was exceeded by EUR 1.6m, mostly through the Kłaipėda yard overrun described in the segment section. The most recent guidance given to investors before year-end, in the Q3 note of October 2023, was revenue of EUR 95m to EUR 98m, EBITDA of EUR 17.0m to EUR 18.0m and net debt to EBITDA of about 3.1x; all three metrics landed inside the range.

## 3. Segment review

Since 2023 the Group reports four segments. They were redefined with effect from 1 January 2024 in the internal reporting (monitoring revenue was previously reported inside Reefer Services), but the 2023 figures here follow the new definitions and have been restated for 2022.

| EUR m | Revenue 2023 | Revenue 2022 | Growth | EBITDA 2023 | EBITDA margin |
| Reefer Services | 52.3 | 47.9 | +9.2% | 8.4 | 16.1% |
| Pharma Lanes | 21.8 | 17.6 | +23.9% | 5.6 | 25.7% |
| Monitoring & Software | 14.6 | 11.5 | +27.0% | 4.3 | 29.5% |
| Yard & Trans-shipment | 8.9 | 7.3 | +21.9% | 1.3 | 14.6% |
| Central costs | — | — | — | -1.7 | — |
| Group | 97.6 | 84.3 | +15.8% | 17.9 | 18.3% |

### 3.1 Reefer Services (Jarek Lindqvist, Head of Operations)

Reefer Services is the original business: we receive reefer containers at the yard, plug them in, watch them, move them, and give them back to the line or the cargo owner. Revenue was EUR 52.3m, up 9.2%, and EBITDA EUR 8.4m (16.1%). Container-days, which is the volume unit that matters, were 1,847,000 against 1,749,000 in 2022, an increase of 5.6%. The average revenue per container-day, including lift moves and surcharges, was EUR 28.32.

Plug capacity is the physical constraint. At year-end the five yards had 6,240 plug points in total — 2,310 at GDY-1, 1,480 at HAM-2, 1,320 at RTM-3, 860 at GOT-4 and 270 at KLJ-5 — and the average number of boxes plugged on any day was 5,060, a utilisation of 81.1%. In the peak weeks of the citrus season utilisation at Gdynia touched 96% and we turned away roughly four hundred box-days, which I would describe as a quality problem rather than a failure but which Rafał, correctly, does not.

Price and mix contributed 2.9 percentage points of growth, volume the rest. Our rate card changed on 1 March 2024 for the new financial year, with an average increase of 3.9%. Customer concentration remains moderate: the largest customer, Fruttoria Baltica, accounted for 9.3% of Group revenue (2022: 10.1%), and the five largest customers together for 28.3%, the next biggest being Helvetia Pharma Logistik GmbH at 7.1%, Nordkjøl Seafood AS at 4.6%, Brunnen & Sohn Frischdienst at 3.9% and Oakhaven Dairy Ltd at 3.4%. The ten largest customers represent 41.8% of revenue.

Operationally the year was dominated by two things: the first full year of the Rotterdam yard under Hanneke Dijkgraaf, where average plugged boxes rose from 880 to 1,240, and the introduction of automated gate-in at GDY-1, which cut the average truck turnaround from 31 to 22 minutes. The gate automation cost EUR 1.69m and is included in the capital expenditure table in section 5.

### 3.2 Pharma Lanes (Benedikta Aaltonen, Quality & Compliance, with Sales)

Pharma Lanes — temperature-controlled lanes for medicinal products under Good Distribution Practice — grew 23.9% to EUR 21.8m with an EBITDA of EUR 5.6m, a margin of 25.7% which is the highest of the three physical segments. We ended the year with 14 active GDP contracts against 11 twelve months earlier. The first of them dates from 2016.

Quality outcomes: 99.64% of pharma consignments were delivered without a temperature excursion outside the validated range, and of the remainder every one was either within the customer's allowable excursion window or reported within the contractual 4 hours. Helvetia Pharma Logistik GmbH audited the Hamburg and Gdynia sites in November 2023 and raised two minor observations (calibration records for two handheld probes; a missing signature on one change control form) which were closed on 19 December 2023. The cost of GDP compliance — validation, calibration, audits, qualified-person time — was EUR 0.31m in the year; part of it is recovered via the GDP-1 temperature mapping charge and the reduced discount cap for pharma customers described in the rate card.

Looking ahead, two lanes for Helvetia from HAM-2 are in contract negotiation as this extract goes to the printer; a signature is expected before the summer, and management has *not* included them in the revenue range for 2024, because experience says contract negotiations with pharmaceutical customers have a way of lasting longer than a Baltic winter.

### 3.3 Monitoring & Software (Tomasz Wrzesiński, CTO)

Revenue EUR 14.6m (+27.0%), EBITDA EUR 4.3m (29.5%). Terse version: more tags, more subscriptions, same cost base, margin up. Longer version below.

- Active KC-Tag 4 units at 31.12.2023: 36,900 (2022: 28,400 incl. KC-Tag 3). Remaining KC-Tag 3 in the field after the handover window closed on 31 October: 4,100, all of which are being swapped on the customer's next visit to a depot.
- Annual recurring revenue (ARR) at year-end: EUR 12.4m, i.e. 85% of segment revenue is recurring; average subscription EUR 28.00 per tag per month, platform included.
- Net revenue retention 111%; gross logo churn 4.1%.
- Tidewalk 6.4 availability 99.93% over twelve months measured at the public API (`api.tidewalk.korvane.example`); the three outages were all hosting-side at Lintu Cloud Hosting and totalled 6 hours 12 minutes.
- Development of Tidewalk v7 started in Q2; EUR 1.31m was capitalised in 2023 under IAS 38 after the criteria were met on 1 June (see note 14 in section 13). The beta is planned for 2025.

Hardware margin on KC-Tag 4 is thin by design — the tag is closer to a razor than a blade — and the batteries come from Voltrac Battery Systems under a framework agreement that expires on 30 June 2026. We took delivery of production batch 1 (22,000 units) in the second quarter and capital tooling for batch 2 was ordered in December.

### 3.4 Yard & Trans-shipment (Dieter Ahlbeck-Roe, COO)

Revenue EUR 8.9m, up 21.9% from EUR 7.3m; EBITDA EUR 1.3m, a margin of 14.6%. The segment sells yard storage, stuffing and unstuffing, and trans-shipment between feeder and mainline vessels. The big event was the opening of the small trans-shipment yard at Kłaipėda (KLJ-5), which started operating in March 2023 and contributed EUR 1.4m of revenue but an EBITDA loss of EUR 0.2m in its first year, against our plan of break-even by the first anniversary. Winter weather and a late delivery of the gate system explain most of the gap. Break-even is now expected in the second quarter of 2024.

At Gothenburg-Arendal the trans-shipment of 93,400 boxes under the agreement with Arendal Port Services was in line with the prior year; we hold the agreement until 31 December 2026 with an option to extend by two years. The segment's EBITDA margin is structurally lower than the other three because of the labour-intensive nature of stuffing work, and management's priority for 2024 is not margin but utilisation.

### 3.5 Fleet and equipment (Gunnar Halvorsrud, Fleet & Maintenance Lead)

I have been asked to keep this to half a page and I have disobeyed in the usual way. The owned pool at 31 December 2023 consisted of 1,312 reefer containers of which 904 are 40-foot high-cube units, 288 are 40-foot standard and 120 are 20-foot, together with 38 yard tractors, 11 reach stackers, 4 straddle-carrier conversions and the usual cloud of forklifts that nobody can count the same way twice. The remainder of the boxes that pass through our yards belong to the shipping lines or to the cargo owners; we are a plug-and-watch business first and an equipment owner second.

Availability of the owned pool measured over the year was 97.2%, against an internal target of 96.5%. The mean time between refrigeration failures was 19,400 operating hours for units fitted since 2019 and 11,800 hours for units installed before 2016 — which is the arithmetic reason for the eight-year refresh cycle the CFO describes in section 5. In 2023 we carried out 3,980 pre-trip inspections on behalf of customers and 1,126 on our own boxes; the PTI failure rate was 4.3% on customer boxes and 2.6% on ours, a gap we attribute to the fact that our own technicians know where the bodies are buried.

The gantry at HAM-2 is overdue for repainting. This has been on the maintenance plan since 2022, and it will be on the plan in 2025 unless somebody finds out how to paint steel in an unbroken run of dry days in Hamburg.

### 3.6 Systems and IT (Kamil Zdunek, IT Support Lead)

The platform landscape is two systems and a lot of glue. Tidewalk is the dispatch and monitoring web application, at version 6.4 at year-end, and ColdLedger is the billing and invoicing module of the ERP from which we have been trying to graduate since roughly the week after we adopted it. ColdLedger processed 61,840 invoices in 2023, an average of 5,153 per month, with a first-time-right rate of 93.5%; the remaining 6.5% were corrected by Finance, which means about 4,020 credit notes and reissues, a number that does not make anybody happy.

The IT estate comprises 412 managed endpoints (laptops, yard tablets and shared consoles), 62 KC-Hub 200 gateways deployed across the five yards, and a monitoring stack hosted by Lintu Cloud Hosting in two availability zones. IT spent EUR 3.07m in the year including licences and hosting but excluding capitalised development, or 3.1% of revenue. A scoping study for ColdLedger's replacement was completed in October at a cost of EUR 0.06m; the study estimates implementation at EUR 2.8m over eighteen months and recommends a go-live outside the peak season. The Board has not yet been asked to approve it.

### 3.7 Pricing and contract terms

Most customer relationships are governed by a framework agreement with a one-page rate schedule drawn from the annual rate card, and by call-offs. Payment terms are 30 days net as a default, 45 or 60 days for large shippers where a contract says so; the weighted average contractual term in 2023 was 36 days, while actual collection took 50.1 days of sales outstanding, which is the gap that Finance's collections team is working on. Volume discounts are tiered on annual container-days, and the average discount actually granted was 4.7% of base day charges, up from 4.1% in 2022, driven by customers moving up a tier. Fuel and energy adjustments are applied quarterly. Contracts with GDP-audited customers carry a cost-recovery element, and about a fifth of Pharma Lanes revenue is under a fixed-price annual contract with a one-time indexation clause every 1 January.

### 3.8 Competitive environment

We compete with three kinds of players and the Board should not mistake them for one another. The first are the large terminal operators, who offer plug-in as an extra on the same invoice as the lift and who can price it below our cost per day if it helps them fill a vessel call; against them we win on dwell-time reporting, on pharma-grade validation and on the fact that our people pick up the phone at 03:00. The second are the regional reefer depots of Baltic and North Sea ports, of which there are around fifteen of meaningful size, and where the contest is mostly on price and plug availability in the peak. The third are the software-only monitoring vendors, with whom we compete only in the narrowest sense, because the KC-Tag 4 sells best attached to a service.

Our estimate of Korvane's share of reefer plug capacity in the Gdynia–Hamburg–Rotterdam corridor is about 7%, based on published capacity figures and our own plug counts; we hold roughly 11% of the Polish market for reefer container handling at the Baltic ports. These are management estimates and have not been verified by an independent party.

