## 4. People and employment costs

At 31 December 2023 the Group employed 301 full-time equivalents (306 persons), compared with 273 FTE a year earlier. By country: Poland 173, Germany 56, the Netherlands 41, Sweden 24 and Lithuania 7. The average number of FTE in the year was 294, so staff costs of EUR 22.6m correspond to roughly EUR 76,900 per average FTE, including employer's social charges, the annual bonus pool and the cost of agency staff converted to a per-FTE equivalent. Voluntary attrition was 8.7% (2022: 11.2%). The Head of People & Culture, Ines Baptista-Okafor, would like it to be noted that this is the lowest figure since 2018 and that the "owls" night shift in Gdynia, historically the most mobile group, contributed no leavers in the last quarter.

Training spend was EUR 0.38m or EUR 1,262 per FTE; the largest component was refrigeration-technician certification (F-gas) for 41 colleagues. The Employee Participation Trust, which holds shares on behalf of staff, distributed EUR 0.15m of dividend to 118 members in June 2023.

Executive directors' remuneration for 2023 totalled EUR 1.96m, of which fixed pay was EUR 1.42m and annual bonus EUR 0.54m. The independent chair of the Board is paid a flat annual fee of EUR 48,000; other non-executive directors waive fees. No options or other long-term incentives were granted in 2023.

## 5. Capital expenditure

Capital expenditure was EUR 16.4m in 2023 (2022: EUR 12.1m), or 16.8% of revenue. The programme is summarised below. As in earlier years about two thirds is growth capex and the rest replaces equipment at end of life.

| Programme | EUR m | Comment |
| Kłaipėda yard KLJ-5 construction and equipment | 3.9 | opened March 2023 |
| Reefer refrigeration unit refresh (Thermaglide Reefer Units BV) | 4.8 | 61 units delivered |
| KC-Tag 4 tooling and production batch 1 | 2.6 | 22,000 units |
| GDY-1 automated gate | 1.69 | live 14 September |
| Tidewalk v7 capitalised development | 1.31 | IAS 38, from 1 June |
| Other and maintenance | 2.10 | |
| Total | 16.4 | |

The Thermaglide programme replaces refrigeration machinery in our owned equipment pool on a rolling eight-year cycle. Thermaglide delivered 61 units in 2023 against 70 ordered; the missing nine arrived in January 2024, which explains why our contingent commitment note shows a purchase obligation of EUR 7.9m at the year end. We intend to order the next tranche in the third quarter of 2024.

## 6. Liquidity, funding and covenants

The Group's borrowing is concentrated with one lender. The senior facilities agreement with Nordhafen Bank AG, Hamburg, dated 12 September 2022, provides a EUR 45.0m amortising term loan with final maturity on 31 October 2029 and a EUR 15.0m revolving credit facility maturing on 31 October 2026. Scheduled repayments on the term loan were EUR 3.5m in 2023, rising to EUR 1.0m per quarter from 1 January 2024, so that the balance at 31 December 2023 was EUR 41.5m. The revolving facility was drawn by EUR 4.0m at the year end. The term loan carries a margin of 1.85% over three-month EURIBOR, the revolving facility 1.65%, and a commitment fee of 0.7% is payable on the undrawn part. The weighted average cost of borrowings was 5.1% in 2023 compared with 3.9% in 2022. All borrowings are floating; no interest-rate hedging was in place at 31 December 2023, although the Board has asked management to bring a proposal in the first half of the year.

Net debt at 31 December 2023 consisted of the term loan of EUR 41.5m, the revolving facility of EUR 4.0m and lease liabilities of EUR 22.6m, less cash of EUR 13.2m. A change of 100 basis points in EURIBOR would change annual interest cost by EUR 0.46m on the floating debt.

There are two financial covenants, tested quarterly on a rolling twelve-month basis. The first is a leverage covenant: net debt (including IFRS 16 lease liabilities) to EBITDA must not exceed 3.75x until and including 31 March 2024, stepping down to 3.50x from 30 June 2024. The second is an interest cover covenant, EBITDA to net finance costs, which must not fall below 4.0x. Both were met at every test date in 2023; at 31 December 2023 the leverage ratio was 3.07x, leaving headroom of 18.2% of EBITDA, and interest cover was 5.8x. Cash at year end was EUR 13.2m and undrawn committed facilities EUR 11.0m, so total liquidity was EUR 24.2m.

Foreign-exchange exposure is modest: 91% of revenue is invoiced in EUR, 6% in SEK and 3% in PLN, whereas about 38% of the cost base is in PLN. A 10% strengthening of the zloty against the euro would reduce EBITDA by about EUR 1.4m on an annual basis. We do not hedge PLN costs at present; the sensitivity is reviewed every quarter by Treasury, which is Priya's team plus one analyst and a very good spreadsheet.

## 7. Principal risks and uncertainties

The Board reviews the risk register twice a year; the following are the risks that in management's view could materially affect the Group in 2024. They are listed in no particular order, and the omission of a risk does not imply that it is regarded as immaterial.

**Customer and cargo concentration.** The Group serves produce, seafood, dairy and pharmaceutical shippers, and the produce season dominates the second half-year. The loss of the largest customer would reduce revenue by 9.3% and EBITDA by a lesser percentage because the fixed yard cost would remain. Customer contracts generally run for twelve to thirty-six months with price review clauses linked to the rate card.

**Equipment availability.** The supply of refrigeration units is concentrated with Thermaglide Reefer Units BV; lead times were 14 weeks at the end of 2023, and management considers a lead time above 18 weeks to be a threat to peak-season readiness. A dual-sourcing study is underway but a second supplier has not yet been qualified.

**Energy.** See section 2.2. A sustained rise of EUR 25 per MWh would erase roughly EUR 1.5m of EBITDA before pass-through via the fuel and energy adjustment.

**Product liability and temperature excursions.** A material excursion on a load of high-value cargo can lead to claims. The Group carries cargo-handler liability insurance through Seaboard Insurance Brokers with a limit of EUR 20m per event and a deductible of EUR 150,000 per claim; the policy renews on 1 January.

**Cyber and systems.** ColdLedger, our billing platform, is an end-of-life customised ERP module whose vendor will cease support in 2027. A replacement project has been scoped but not approved. Tidewalk is hosted with Lintu Cloud Hosting under a contract that includes a 99.9% availability service level.

**Regulation.** The EU F-gas regulation, the revised GDP guidelines and the CSRD reporting duties, which become applicable to the Group for the financial year 2026 on the current timetable, all increase compliance cost.

## 8. Corporate governance (extract)

The Board consists of five directors: Ulrika Stenmark-Doyle as independent chair, Marta Kowarzik, Dieter Ahlbeck-Roe, Priya Venkataraman-Holt and Dr Bartosz Kielich, who was nominated by Baltic Growth Partners. The Chief Technology Officer, Tomasz Wrzesiński, attends by invitation but is not a director. The Board held seven meetings in 2023, with an attendance rate of 94%. The Audit Committee, which is chaired by Ulrika Stenmark-Doyle with Dr Kielich as its other member, met four times; the external auditor attended three of those meetings without management present for part of the agenda.

The shareholders at 31 December 2023 were Marta Kowarzik (31.5%), Dieter Ahlbeck-Roe (29.5%), Baltic Growth Partners (24.0%) and the Employee Participation Trust (15.0%). A shareholders' agreement of 3 March 2021 contains tag-along and drag-along provisions and a reserved-matters list, in particular any single investment above EUR 5.0m and any change to the dividend policy, which requires consent from shareholders holding at least 75% of the votes.

The financial statements were audited by Aldermoor Kessler Audit Sp. z o.o., with Henrik Szymański-Dahl as the key statutory auditor, which issued an unmodified opinion dated 12 April 2024. Audit fees for the year were EUR 148,000, plus EUR 22,000 for non-audit services (agreed-upon procedures on covenant calculations). The Audit Committee has decided that non-audit fees may not exceed 30% of audit fees. A separate management letter containing control observations will be issued to the Board after the date of this report.

## 9. Geography and customer sectors

Management reports its depots as the primary geographic unit, since the cost base and the customer relationship are local even though the contracts are usually written in Gdynia. The table assigns revenue to the country in which the service is delivered; the subscription revenue of Monitoring & Software is shown on its own line because a Tidewalk user in Rotterdam may be a Hamburg customer's subsidiary and we have long given up trying to untangle that in ColdLedger.

| EUR m | 2023 | 2022 | Change |
| Poland (GDY-1) | 30.1 | 27.6 | +9.1% |
| Germany (HAM-2) | 20.9 | 19.4 | +7.7% |
| Netherlands (RTM-3) | 17.1 | 14.0 | +22.1% |
| Sweden (GOT-4) | 12.3 | 11.8 | +4.2% |
| Lithuania (KLJ-5) | 2.6 | — | n/m |
| Monitoring & Software subscriptions | 14.6 | 11.5 | +27.0% |
| Group | 97.6 | 84.3 | +15.8% |

The Netherlands grew fastest because 2022 contained only ten months of operations at the Maasvlakte, whereas the Gothenburg depot, our most mature, has little room left to grow inside the current footprint and added a modest 4.2%. Gdynia grew 9.1% and the depot manager, Jarek, wants it recorded that this was achieved while the gate was being rebuilt around the trucks. The Lithuanian figure of EUR 2.6m consists of EUR 1.4m trans-shipment and yard services and EUR 1.2m of plug rental for boxes waiting for feeder connections.

By cargo sector, fresh produce (citrus, berries, bananas, vegetables) made up 46% of container-days, seafood 17%, dairy and chilled food 19%, pharmaceuticals and other temperature-sensitive healthcare 11% by container-days but 22% of revenue, and a residual 7% of flowers, chemicals and project cargo. The point is that a pharma box earns about twice as much per day as a banana box, and a cautious reader of these numbers should be careful about concluding anything about volume from revenue mix alone.

## 10. Sustainability, energy and safety

We have not yet adopted the full CSRD reporting framework, but we collect the data. Total electricity consumption was 58,300 MWh, of which 41% was covered by guarantees of origin for renewable energy; the Rotterdam yard is supplied 100% from certified renewable sources and Gdynia is the weak point at 22%. Scope 1 emissions (yard diesel and company vehicles) were 3,120 tonnes CO2e. Scope 2 emissions were 12,450 tonnes CO2e on a market-based basis and 21,800 tonnes on a location-based basis. The energy intensity of our core service, expressed as kWh of electricity per plugged container-day, was 31.6 kWh. Management's target is to bring this down to 29.0 kWh per container-day by the end of 2026 through better insulation of the plug racks, smarter defrost scheduling in the KC-Hub 200 gateways, and the retirement of the oldest refrigeration units.

Refrigerant top-ups in our owned equipment pool amounted to 183 kg of R-513A, a leakage rate of 2.4% of the installed charge, which is within the 3.0% tolerance in our maintenance standard. The Group worked 586,000 hours in 2023 and recorded three lost-time injuries, a rate of 5.1 per million hours worked; none occurred at GDY-1. Gunnar Halvorsrud's fleet team has proposed, and the Board has accepted in principle, a programme to replace diesel yard tractors with electric ones as they reach end of life, starting with six units in 2025.

The Quality & Compliance function maintains certification to ISO 9001 and ISO 22000 for the Gdynia, Hamburg-Waltershof and Rotterdam-Maasvlakte sites. The surveillance audit in September 2023 produced no major non-conformities and four minor ones; the next full recertification audit is scheduled for the autumn of 2024.

## 11. Treasury, insurance and tax

Treasury policy is to keep liquidity, defined as cash plus undrawn committed facilities, above EUR 15.0m at all times and above 12% of annual revenue at each quarter-end; at 31 December 2023 the figures were EUR 24.2m and 24.8%. Surplus cash is held at relationship banks with a minimum rating of A-, with no single counterparty holding more than EUR 8.0m, except for the operating accounts of Nordhafen Bank AG, which are under a separate set-off arrangement. Foreign currency borrowing is not permitted without Board approval.

Insurance is placed through Seaboard Insurance Brokers. In addition to the cargo-handler liability policy mentioned in the risk section (EUR 20m per event, EUR 150,000 deductible), the Group carries property damage cover for a replacement value of EUR 118m, business interruption cover with a twelve-month indemnity period, and a cyber policy with a limit of EUR 5.0m and a retention of EUR 75,000. The total premium for 2023 was EUR 1.34m, which is 1.4% of revenue and about 12% more than in 2022 after the market hardened for cold-storage risks.

On tax, the effective rate of 25.4% reflects Polish, German, Dutch, Swedish and Lithuanian taxable profits and the non-recognition of a deferred tax asset of EUR 1.9m on Swedish tax losses carried forward, which management does not yet consider probable of utilisation. Income tax paid in cash was EUR 1.2m. A German tax audit of the Hamburg entity covering 2019 to 2021 was concluded in November 2023 with additional tax of EUR 86,000 plus interest, which was charged to the 2023 result.

## 12. Related parties, going concern and events after the reporting date

*Related parties.* The head office at ul. Łużycka 14 is leased from Ahlbeck-Roe Nieruchomości Sp. z o.o., a company controlled by Dieter Ahlbeck-Roe, at a rent of EUR 31,400 per month, i.e. EUR 376,800 for the year. The lease runs until 31 March 2030; the rent was benchmarked against an independent valuation in 2022, and Dieter abstained when the Board approved the last indexation. There were no other transactions with directors or shareholders in the year except for dividends and remuneration.

*Going concern.* The directors have assessed the Group's ability to continue as a going concern for at least twelve months from the date of approval. In the downside case, in which revenue is 12% below the 2024 budget and EBITDA 22% below, liquidity remains positive throughout and both covenants are met with limited headroom in the first half of 2025. The directors therefore consider it appropriate to prepare the financial statements on a going-concern basis.

*Events after the reporting date.* Remaining nine refrigeration units from the 2023 Thermaglide order were delivered in January 2024. A 36-month contract with Lumikko Foods Oy started on 1 February 2024. Release 6.5 of Tidewalk went live on 21 February 2024. The new rate card entered into force on 1 March 2024. No adjusting events occurred between the reporting date and the date of approval.

## 13. Extract from the notes to the financial statements

### Note 2 — Significant accounting policies (excerpt)

*Revenue.* Day charges for plugged and stored containers are recognised over time, per started day, as the service is provided. Lift moves, gate-in handling and pre-trip inspections are recognised at the point in time at which the move or inspection is complete. Subscription revenue for KC-Tag 4 and Tidewalk is recognised monthly over the contract period; hardware delivered as part of a bundle is not accounted for separately because it is not distinct. Variable consideration, mainly volume discounts, is estimated using the expected value method and recognised as a reduction of revenue, and a refund liability of EUR 0.64m was recorded at 31 December 2023 for discounts not yet credited.

*Property, plant and equipment.* Depreciation is straight-line over the following useful lives: plug racks and electrical installations 12 years; refrigeration units 8 years; reach stackers and yard tractors 10 years; KC-Tag 4 devices rented to customers 6 years; buildings 25 years. Right-of-use assets are depreciated over the lease term. The incremental borrowing rate used to discount lease payments was 5.4% for leases commencing in 2023.

*Development costs.* Costs of developing software are capitalised from the date on which the technical feasibility, the intention and ability to complete, and the probable future benefit have been demonstrated. For Tidewalk v7 these conditions were judged to be met on 1 June 2023; costs incurred before that date, EUR 0.27m, were expensed.

### Note 14 — Intangible assets (excerpt)

Intangible assets of EUR 7.6m comprise capitalised development costs of EUR 3.9m, software licences of EUR 2.2m, and customer-related intangibles acquired with the 2019 purchase of the Gothenburg business of EUR 1.5m, the latter with a remaining amortisation period of 5 years. The Monitoring & Software cash-generating unit was tested for impairment using a discount rate of 9.1% after tax and a terminal growth rate of 2.0%; the recoverable amount exceeded the carrying amount by EUR 11.3m.

### Note 22 — Provisions (excerpt)

Provisions at 31 December 2023 were EUR 1.18m. The largest items are a warranty provision for KC-Tag 4 of EUR 0.37m, calculated on a 12-month warranty and an observed return rate of 1.9% of units sold, an onerous-lease provision of EUR 0.41m for the surplus Rotterdam warehouse space that Korvane hopes to sub-let, and a provision for holiday pay and bonus of EUR 0.40m. No provision has been recognised for contingent liabilities relating to customer claims in excess of EUR 0.25m, because none were pending at the reporting date.

### Note 31 — Commitments (excerpt)

Purchase commitments for capital expenditure contracted but not yet delivered at 31 December 2023 amounted to EUR 7.9m, mostly refrigeration units and the second KC-Tag batch tooling. Future minimum lease payments under non-cancellable leases of low-value assets and short-term leases not recognised on the balance sheet were EUR 0.63m.

## 14. Outlook 2024, dividend and calendar

Our guidance for 2024 is as follows. Revenue is expected to be in the range of EUR 104m to EUR 108m, EBITDA between EUR 19.0m and EUR 20.0m (an EBITDA margin of 18% to 19%), and capital expenditure not above EUR 19.5m. Net debt to EBITDA is expected to be below 3.0x at the end of 2024 and the Group expects to remain in compliance with both covenants. The guidance does not include the potential effects of the contracts under negotiation described in section 3.2.

The Board proposes a dividend of EUR 1.5m for 2023, which is 31.9% of net profit, to be considered by the Ordinary Shareholders' Meeting on 27 May 2024, with payment on 20 June 2024. The dividend policy remains a payout ratio of 25% to 35% of net profit.

Financial calendar: Q1 2024 management accounts to the lenders by 15 May 2024; half-year results at the Board meeting on 28 June 2024; Q3 figures in October.

## 15. Statement of the Board

The Board confirms that, to the best of its knowledge, the annual financial statements give a true and fair view of the assets, liabilities, financial position and result of the Company and that the management report presents a fair review of the development, achievements and position of the Company together with the principal risks. The statement is made on behalf of the Board by the President of the Management Board and the Chief Financial Officer.

